-CI is new: the repo has no CI config yet (A23). `.github/workflows/check.yml` runs two independent jobs on every PR — `rust` (the three gate commands above, with nothing installed beyond the pinned toolchain (A11)) and `orchestration` (`npm ci && npm run check` in `tools/orchestration`, whose `setup-node` `node-version` is pinned to `22.23.4` — the Node floor, i.e. the version `tools/orchestration` already builds under, taken verbatim from its `engines.node` entry (A28); this is the only Node floor this design sets, and the `repo-practices` Rust section documents that same value) — satisfying the ADR's "CI must run both". Per-PR dependency-advisory scanning is deliberately excluded from the gate: it needs a tool outside the pinned toolchain and an advisory database that is live network state, so it can turn an unchanged PR red on any day the database moves. That obligation is not deferred — this design is the document that creates the repo's CI, so it keeps the only recurring look at the lockfile it commits: a second workflow, `.github/workflows/advisories.yml` (Rollout 4), runs weekly and on manual dispatch, never as a PR check, installing `cargo-deny` at a pinned version and running `cargo deny check advisories` against the committed `Cargo.lock` and a root `deny.toml`; the repo maintainer triages what it reports within the week, and a `deny.toml` `skip` names the advisory id it waives. That same run is the trigger for the `rust-toolchain.toml` security-advisory bump (Workspace shape); its crates.io/RustSec data is not every dependency's only advisory source (A27, TypeScript boundary). Both workflows declare `permissions: contents: read` at the job level and pin every third-party action to a commit SHA. No job needs Postgres; the sqlx offline-cache mechanism decided in Options considered arrives with `apps/hub` under ADR-014 (see Deferred).
